@startuml proto-dht11-frame
' ===========================================================================
' proto-dht11-frame.puml - (b) the DHT11 40-bit frame.
'
' A DHT11 frame is five bytes, MSB first, with no header and no length:
'   byte 0  humidity, integer part    (0x2C = 44 -> 40 % RH)
'   byte 1  humidity, decimal part
'   byte 2  temperature, integer part (0x01 = 1 -> 40.0 C)
'   byte 3  temperature, decimal part
'   byte 4  CHECKSUM = (b0 + b1 + b2 + b3) & 0xff
'
' Every one of the 40 bits is a WIDTH, not a level. The bit value IS the length
' of the sensor's release, so the host has to be at a particular instant in each
' bit rather than reading a level.
' ===========================================================================

title DHT11 - the 40-bit frame\n<font size=12>five bytes, MSB first; each bit is a WIDTH, not a level</font>


hide empty description
hide stereotype
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam rectangle {
  BorderColor #33555
  FontName monospaced
  FontSize 12
}
skinparam rectangle<<b>> {
  BackgroundColor #F4F7FB
}
skinparam rectangle<<zero>> {
  BackgroundColor #E8E8E8
}
skinparam rectangle<<gate>> {
  BackgroundColor #FFF4D6
  BorderColor #C8B800
}

rectangle "START\n\n<b>18 093 us</b> measured\n<b>18 000 us</b> required\n\nthe sensor's reset" as S <<gate>>
rectangle "HOST WINDOW\n\n<b>30.0 us</b> measured\n<b>20 .. 40 us</b> required\n\ndriven HIGH, then\nRELEASED on pin_oe" as H <<gate>>
rectangle "ACK\n\n<b>80 us</b> LOW\n<b>80 us</b> HIGH\n\nNEVER TIMED by the\nfirmware - skipped by\nthe edge-wait loop" as A <<gate>>

rectangle "BYTE 0\nHUMIDITY, integer\n\n<b>0x2C</b> = 0010 1100\n= 44 -> <b>40 % RH</b>\n\n40 % x 0xFF is the\nmodelling shortcut the\nTB uses for the integer" as B0 <<b>>
rectangle "BYTE 1\nHUMIDITY, decimal\n\n<b>0x01</b> = 0000 0001" as B1 <<b>>
rectangle "BYTE 2\nTEMPERATURE, integer\n\n<b>0x01</b> = 0000 0001\n= 1 -> <b>40.0 C</b>" as B2 <<b>>
rectangle "BYTE 3\nTEMPERATURE, decimal\n\n<b>0xAA</b> = 1010 1010" as B3 <<b>>
rectangle "CHECKSUM\n\n<b>0x2C</b>\n\n0x2C + 0x01 + 0xAA + 0x55\n= 0x12C, and 0x12C & 0xFF = <b>0x2C</b>\n\n<b>the sensor sends it; the</b>\n<b>firmware RECORDS it and</b>\n<b>does not check it - the</b>\n<b>testbench does</b>" as CK <<b>>
rectangle "BUS HOLD\n\n<b>50 us</b>\n\nthen the line is\nreleased and the\nsensor is left alone" as BH <<gate>>

S -[#3A7]-> H
H -[#3A7]-> A
A -[#3A7]-> B0 : 8 bits
B0 -[#3A7]-> B1
B1 -[#3A7]-> B2
B2 -[#3A7]-> B3
B3 -[#3A7]-> CK
CK -[#3A7]-> BH
BH -[#3A7,dashed]-> S : a real driver loops;\nthis one reads once and parks

note bottom of S
  <b>The start signal is the longest single timed interval in the project.</b>
  18 ms = 1.08 million clocks - 6.4 times the WS2812 reset and 900 times the
  WS2812 bit cell - so it is what forces a three-level delay routine rather than
  a NOP sled, and it is on the (6,255) pair whose outer step is 5 145 clocks
  = 85.8 us.
end note

note bottom of H
  <b>Why the host PULLS HIGH before releasing, which is not optional.</b>

  The DHT11 pulls its own line LOW to answer, so the
  host cannot simply stop driving and let the pull-up
  win: the sensor would see a line that never went
  high and would not start converting. Pulling it high
  for 30 us guarantees a clean high-to-low transition
  to answer with, and only then does the host become a
  reader.

  It is also the one place this program drives the line
  high push-pull into a device that may pull it low,
  which is safe only because the host has already
  stopped driving by the time the sensor answers.
end note

note bottom of A
  <b>Why the acknowledgement is skipped rather than timed.</b>

  Nothing on the wire announces when the acknowledge is
  over, so timing it would be another delay constant to
  fit and another place to be wrong. Instead the firmware
  falls into the same edge-wait loop it uses for bits:
  the first round waits out the 80/80 us acknowledge and
  the second one is bit 0. One loop, two uses.
end note

note bottom of CK
  <b>Why the firmware records the checksum instead of checking it.</b>

  A program that silently drops a bad frame is a program
  whose failure is invisible - it looks like a sensor
  that has gone quiet. So the byte is banked in dmem[4]
  and the CHECK is left to whoever reads it, here the
  testbench. The firmware's job is to be faithful to the
  wire; the decision about what to do about it belongs
  to the layer above.
end note

legend bottom
  |= bit |= sensor: low | sensor: release | total | the host samples at 45 us into the release |=
  | <b>0</b> | 50 us | <b>28 us</b> (the SHORT end of the 26-28 us window - a sensor inside its own spec) | 78 us | the line is already LOW again: 17 us of margin |
  | <b>1</b> | 50 us | <b>70 us</b> | 120 us | the line is still HIGH: 25 us of margin |

  <b>The bit PERIOD is not fixed</b> - 76..78 us for a 0 and 120 us for a 1 - and that is
  the whole reason the host synchronises on edges instead of counting. At the 2 us
  spread of the 0-bit, twenty zero bits is 40 us of slip against a 17 us margin: a
  counting host tracks the nominal frame perfectly and then fails on a sensor that
  is inside its own specification.

  <b>THE SAMPLE CANNOT GO INSIDE THE RELEASE.</b> A line high 20 us into the release
  is a 0 AND a 1. The two differ only afterwards, so the sample is aimed at the
  45 us point where a 0 has already ended and a 1 has not.

  <b>There is no 9th bit, no parity and no framing.</b> Forty bits, five bytes, and
  an 8-bit sum.
end legend

@enduml
